WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:07.945
In this film you read the portfolio of a fund, follow a position to its master data, and see how the schema protects that link.

2
00:00:07.995 --> 00:00:09.539
It takes about six minutes.

3
00:00:10.392 --> 00:00:13.677
The portfolio is part of the fund's dynamic data.

4
00:00:13.727 --> 00:00:20.824
Portfolios can hold several portfolios, each with a NAV date, the valuation day, and a list of positions.

5
00:00:21.476 --> 00:00:25.581
Each position starts with a UniqueID, and identifiers like the ISIN.

6
00:00:25.631 --> 00:00:34.562
Then come its currency, here the US dollar, its total value in the fund currency, and its weight: one point two one percent of the net asset value.

7
00:00:34.612 --> 00:00:39.328
For a position in a foreign currency, the exchange rate used is given as well.

8
00:00:39.378 --> 00:00:42.322
The last block depends on the kind of instrument.

9
00:00:42.372 --> 00:00:48.737
For a share, it holds the number of units, the price in dollars, and the market value in both currencies.

10
00:00:49.892 --> 00:00:52.051
Every position has the same shape.

11
00:00:52.101 --> 00:00:57.575
A common head: the link to the asset, its value in the fund currency, and its weight.

12
00:00:57.625 --> 00:01:08.948
Then exactly one block for the kind of instrument, chosen from about twenty: equity, bond, fund units, options, futures, currency forwards, swaps, deposits, accounts and more.

13
00:01:08.998 --> 00:01:15.576
The quantity sits inside that block: units for a share, a nominal for a bond, contracts for an option.

14
00:01:16.826 --> 00:01:20.722
A bond has a nominal value and a price in percent instead.

15
00:01:20.772 --> 00:01:23.239
A fund unit holds a number of shares.

16
00:01:23.289 --> 00:01:25.007
An option holds contracts.

17
00:01:25.057 --> 00:01:29.089
And cash, here call money, needs hardly more than its value.

18
00:01:30.204 --> 00:01:33.744
What a security is, is not repeated in every position.

19
00:01:33.794 --> 00:01:37.600
The UniqueID points to the asset master data further down.

20
00:01:37.650 --> 00:01:47.705
There, asset ID_0002 is described once: Driftwood Engineering, a fictional share from the United States, listed in New York, with the LEI of its issuer.

21
00:01:47.755 --> 00:01:53.821
If several funds or portfolios hold the same share, its master data still appears only once.

22
00:01:54.970 --> 00:01:57.059
An asset has its own fixed shape.

23
00:01:57.109 --> 00:01:58.809
The UniqueID is the anchor.

24
00:01:58.859 --> 00:02:12.418
Then come its identifiers and its name, its currency, and the asset type, a two-letter code: E Q for equity, B O for bond, S C for a fund's share class, O P for an option, F X for a currency forward, and so on.

25
00:02:12.468 --> 00:02:22.268
Asset details hold what is specific to the kind: the issuer of a share, the coupon of a bond, the strike of an option, or the counterparty of a forward.

26
00:02:23.518 --> 00:02:26.022
Now a bond, from the position to its asset.

27
00:02:26.072 --> 00:02:32.851
The position holds a nominal of six point two million euro, at a price of one hundred five point three six percent.

28
00:02:32.901 --> 00:02:39.206
Nominal times price gives the market value, and the interest accrued since the last coupon is added on top.

29
00:02:39.256 --> 00:02:52.627
On the asset side, the bond is described once: a government bond of a fictional state, with its issuer and the issuer's LEI, the maturity date in October twenty forty-five, and a fixed coupon of three point seven five percent.

30
00:02:53.279 --> 00:02:55.914
And a fund unit, here an exchange-traded fund.

31
00:02:55.964 --> 00:03:00.524
The position holds about forty-one thousand shares at eighty-three euro eighty.

32
00:03:00.574 --> 00:03:07.061
The asset is of type S C, a share class of another fund, with the management company of that fund as its issuer.

33
00:03:07.111 --> 00:03:14.005
This is the starting point for look-through: the target fund can be delivered in the same file, with its own portfolio.

34
00:03:14.657 --> 00:03:16.224
Here is a currency forward.

35
00:03:16.274 --> 00:03:26.471
On the asset side: buy about twenty-one million dollars, sell about eighteen million euro, at an agreed rate, with maturity in December, and the counterparty with its LEI.

36
00:03:26.521 --> 00:03:32.703
On the position side, the total value is the forward's current market value, which can also be negative.

37
00:03:33.852 --> 00:03:37.499
Technically, this is a link the XML parser itself checks.

38
00:03:37.549 --> 00:03:43.012
On the asset, UniqueID is declared as an ID: it must be unique in the whole document.

39
00:03:43.062 --> 00:03:47.682
On the position, it is an ID reference: it must point to an existing ID.

40
00:03:47.732 --> 00:03:52.222
The value must be a valid XML name, so it must not start with a digit.

41
00:03:52.272 --> 00:03:58.384
A good habit is a stable value: the same asset keeps the same ID from one delivery to the next.

42
00:03:59.634 --> 00:04:01.391
The schema protects this link.

43
00:04:01.441 --> 00:04:06.693
Change the UniqueID of the first position to an asset that does not exist, and press F8.

44
00:04:06.743 --> 00:04:10.006
The schema reports that there is no asset with this ID.

45
00:04:10.657 --> 00:04:14.496
Set the ID back, validate again, and the document is in order.

46
00:04:15.649 --> 00:04:23.537
A portfolio can also carry breakdowns: the fund's weights by country, currency, sector or asset class, already calculated by the sender.

47
00:04:23.587 --> 00:04:29.140
A breakdown can combine several dimensions, for example country and currency, and can be nested.

48
00:04:29.190 --> 00:04:34.218
The receiver can use them as they are, and should allow for small rounding differences.

49
00:04:34.268 --> 00:04:42.331
Breakdowns are useful for receivers who only need an overview, such as a factsheet, and do not want to aggregate every position themselves.

50
00:04:43.681 --> 00:04:45.552
And funds that hold other funds?

51
00:04:45.602 --> 00:04:49.155
A position of the kind ShareClass points to the target fund.

52
00:04:49.205 --> 00:04:54.921
The target fund can be delivered in the same file, as a fund of its own, with its own portfolio.

53
00:04:54.971 --> 00:05:03.028
And a portfolio can also carry decomposed positions, where fund holdings are replaced by the scaled-down positions of the target funds.

54
00:05:03.078 --> 00:05:09.874
That is how look-through works in FundsXML: through the fund hierarchy, not through extra fields on each position.

55
00:05:11.224 --> 00:05:13.987
That was the portfolio and its master data.

56
00:05:14.037 --> 00:05:18.894
In the next film, you see how transactions and investor flows are recorded.

57
00:05:18.944 --> 00:05:20.203
Thanks for watching.
